Point 53 (v0.0.71 SHIPPED: Peak NPS -- the honest, BPM-independent physical-difficulty number -- a NEW system)\
v0.0.71 ships PEAK NPS, a NEW system that is the physical-load twin of the v0.0.56-70 green-number (reading-load) engine. The green number tells you how long you have to READ a note; NPS tells you how many notes your hands actuate per second. Motivation: an inventory of every existing analysis/stats feature confirmed all density views are TICK-based -- the per-measure Density Heatmap (heatmap.js), the per-beat Adaptive-Compress scan (tools.js), and computeChartStats 'peak' (notes-per-measure, chart.js) -- so a busy measure at 200 BPM scores the same as the identical measure at 100 BPM even though it is twice as physically hard. NPS fixes this by measuring notes per REAL wall-clock second.\
Implementation: a single DOM-free, unit-tested source of truth chart.notesPerSecond(opts) that collects every BT and FX onset tick (a hold counts once at its start, matching computeChartStats' buckets; lasers are excluded as continuous knob motion = the Tsumami axis), converts each onset to seconds through the existing tickToSeconds BPM-integrated time model, and runs a two-pointer sweep over the sorted onset times with a fixed windowSec (default 1.0s) to return peakNps / peakTick / peakTime / meanNps / total / spanSec. The sweep is provably exact because the max-count window can always be slid left until its left edge meets an onset without dropping any note. app.js updateNpsReadout() (called from render()'s coalesced frame, cached on a cheap onset-signature so it no-ops when the notes are unchanged) paints #pvc-nps and seeks to the densest burst on click; toggleNpsReadout + prefs.npsReadout persist it. index.html adds a preview 'Density' section; i18n preview.density / nps / npsTip in all 5 locales. Render-only -- the chart is never mutated; no KSON/data-format risk.\
Verified: 18 node ESM unit tests (empty -> zeros; 120bpm quarters -> 2 NPS; the SAME tick pattern at 240 BPM -> exactly DOUBLE the NPS, the BPM-independence proof a tick metric fails; 4-note chord -> 4 NPS in one instant; FX + a measure-long hold each counted as one onset; an 8-note burst's peak anchored at its start tick; windowSec 0.5/2.0 scaling; soflan peak lands in the fast half; determinism; no mutation) and a real-browser Playwright run on a soflan .ksh (sparse 120-BPM measure then a dense 16-note 240-BPM measure): NPS toggle on -> readout 16.0 amber, seekTick 192, tooltip '16.0 NPS peak, 7.0 avg, 21 notes over 3s', click-to-seek, npsReadout persisted, reload restores the active toggle, ZERO real JS pageerrors -- matching the hand-computed 16 NPS. Status: SHIPPED in v0.0.71.\
