ng is NOT double-counted. Each action is stamped to seconds via the shared tickToSeconds time model and the same provably-exact onset-anchored 1-second sliding window notesPerSecond uses finds the peak, returning peakKps / peakTick / meanKps / total / slams / reversals / grabs; BPM-independent by construction (16th slams -> 8/s at 120 BPM, exactly 16/s at 240). (2) a new exported chart.knobDifficultyBand(peakKps) bands it None / Light (>0) / Moderate (>=4) / Busy (>=8) / Heavy (>=12) / Extreme (>=18) calibrated to wrist load, with a stable key + colour matching the difficulty-band palette. (3) computeChartStats folds the result in beside Hand Balance (guarded typeof chart.knobStress === 'function' so a plain-object caller degrades to zero, never throws), exporting peakKps / meanKps / peakKnobTick / knobSlams / knobReversals / knobGrabs / knobTotal. (4) a new 'Knob Load (per second)' row in BOTH the Chart Statistics modal (app.js) and the Tools-Hub Chart Statistics tool (tools.js) shows the peak knob-actions/s, the band label/colour and the slam + reversal breakdown, driven by the shared band so the two can never disagree. Render-only -- the chart is never mutated; no KSON/data-format risk. Tested: 41 node ESM unit tests pass (empty -> None/0, no throw; a single slam -> 1 grab + 1 slam; a slow zig-zag -> 1 grab + 1 reversal; a slam-that-is-also-a-reversal counted ONCE; both laser sides counted; sub-eps position wiggle is neither slam nor reversal; the BPM-independence proof -- 16th slams DOUBLE from 120 to 240 BPM; computeChartStats fold-in exactly equals a direct knobStress call; a plain-object caller does NOT throw; every band boundary at 0/4/8/12/18 and NaN/negative; no mutation). Two real-browser Playwright runs: the live app computed a 32-slam 16th storm at 180 BPM -> peakKps 13, 31 slams, band Heavy with the computeChartStats fold-in matching, and opened the Chart Statistics modal in the live UI to confirm the new row renders under Hand Balance in the Density b